#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068617 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e9590de-9413-5bec-b3f4-315038a3848e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "3a0a00c8-8a86-52de-bc9d-d9219a0735cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-591" :question/problem "![](images/73/7373418f2a0bac416e37662cea5a79101220820aa6f23c50a80fbf6addc5eefd.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y=x^2$ and $y=x^5$, as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\nand $g(x)=x^5$.\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= g(x) \\\\\nx^{2} &= x^{5} \\\\\nx^{5} - x^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2}(x^{3} - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$.\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/e4/e41991c460504023c4086aa0070904f20d88304aecd42a293c4b0422132b40e0.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)=x^5$, and\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=x^2$.\n\nTherefore, the volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{a}^{b}([outer]^{2} - [inner]^{2})dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}([x^{2}]^{2} - [x^{5}]^{2})dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}(x^{4} - x^{10})dx \\\\\n&= \\left.π(\\frac{x^{5}}{5} - \\frac{x^{11}}{11})\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= π[(\\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{11}) - 0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{6π}{55}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068618 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7144a491-71ac-565e-9687-3c8ed0a0c0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "a22887cf-cafa-5ba5-9834-e0c435ec86af" :question/math-academy-id "e-592" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd7319762569fbab0e64f03719b41af9dfb45d07aac073a534ea68f842b1ce99.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $x=\\sqrt{4-y^2}$, $x=1$, and the horizontal lines $y=-1$ and $y=1$, as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\sqrt{4-y^2}\\,\n$$\n\nand $g(y)=1$.\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7f/7f875c024b25c37c434a82a4320b4927bde85dc8f0ada3b35837330f829b23c7.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=1$, and\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt{4-y^2}$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{c}^{d}([outer]^{2} - [inner]^{2})dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{1}([\\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}]^{2} - 1^{2})dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{1}(3 - y^{2})dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}(3 - y^{2})dy \\\\\n&= \\left.2π(3y - \\frac{y^{3}}{3})\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 2π(3 - \\frac{1}{3} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{16π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068645 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04fd7672-4a22-5149-a97f-8910845101b0" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "c8be6b12-aad0-5662-a1ae-fa509f578718" :question/math-academy-id "e-7314" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f6ee4ef4870da694c0ef56cf7395012c9ea1edbe4bc31247d9cdc7b26186b7db.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and $y=x^3$, as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nand $g(x)=x^3$.\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= g(x) \\\\\n\\sqrt{x} &= x^{3} \\\\\nx &= x^{6} \\\\\nx^{6} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{5} - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$.\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/2a/2abe3a274ab5afee2bc0a85c48c2f1218620b86354c3c4e6e4dd8758ca1af5e5.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)=x^3$, and\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{a}^{b}([outer]^{2} - [inner]^{2})dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}([\\sqrt{x}]^{2} - [x^{3}]^{2})dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}(x - x^{6})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068646 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2e4ab04-30ed-5e74-88e2-e30626b46312" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "de8fda0f-8a0f-5f6e-94ab-3852771288db" :question/math-academy-id "e-7315" :question/problem "![](images/97/97ead66fca615e8f9325d445ca14a8478a276e6dc8d2878a2fd5dbea5d880f7a.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y=x^3$ and $x=y$, where $y \\leq 0$, as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Writing our first function in terms of $y$, we get\n\n$$\ny = x^{3}⟹x = \\sqrt[3]{y}\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}\n$$\n\nand $g(y)=y$.\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(y) &= g(y) \\\\\n\\sqrt[3]{y} &= y \\\\\ny &= y^{3} \\\\\ny^{3} - y &= 0 \\\\\ny(y^{2} - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $y=0$ and $y=\\pm 1$. But, since\n\n$$\ny \\leq 0\n$$\n\nthe limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=-1$.\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/1a/1ab4ce88964c1a4ce12fdfb93b77810b675bc18e656060629c3544eea47129b2.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=y$, and\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{a}^{b}([outer]^{2} - [inner]^{2})dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{0}([\\sqrt[3]{y}]^{2} - [y]^{2})dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{0}(\\sqrt[3]{y^{2}} - y^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04fd7672-4a22-5149-a97f-8910845101b0" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81eabebd-da65-5b35-afc2-ac9dad426e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(1 - x)^{4}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065518} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3d0acf-c318-5b7b-9a29-04d371acee8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}([1 - x^{2}]^{2} - [1 - x]^{2})dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065519} {:answer/id #uuid "305e10cd-f7d5-5633-826f-295873a523e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}([1 - x]^{4} - [1 - x^{2}]^{2})dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065520} {:answer/id #uuid "33fa9f8e-e715-5d4d-be64-27f499f4a72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}([1 - x^{2}]^{2} - [1 - x]^{4})dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065521} {:answer/id #uuid "4fa13714-7408-5f81-a0d6-a759136b62af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(1 - x)^{2}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065522}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c20b2b4b-311d-5118-a601-de5eb214ef9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065517}] :question/id #uuid "bd1790e6-f31f-5064-a1ab-c0c1da6fc5dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-11096" :question/problem "![](images/4e/4eaf3eac9364aca72506f0d11c7d69332bbf853871d85986e45bd3082a607354.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $y = 1 - x^{2}$ and $y = x^{2} - 2x + 1$, as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7144a491-71ac-565e-9687-3c8ed0a0c0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8643c0df-ab14-5292-86d2-158a0854b9f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5π}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065524} {:answer/id #uuid "ac624d89-25ce-5aff-8a15-18fb907cedab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065525} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d43040-346c-5a6d-9b66-80aad